CS 214 -- Project I: My little malloc()
Authors:
    Agustin Blaumann -- ab3211
    Subhashis Srivastava -- ss4694

BUILD AND RUN

    make            Build the programs.
    ./memgrind      Run the stress workload.
    make test       Run all tests.
    make clean      Remove build files.

FILES AND PROGRAMS

mymalloc.c implements the allocator using mymalloc.h. memtest checks payload
contents; memtest-leak checks leak reporting. correctness tests allocation and
error handling; check.sh checks diagnostics and exit statuses. These programs
need no arguments except correctness, whose optional mode is valid (default),
bounds, exhaustion, fragmentation, stack, interior, header, double,
coalesced-double, null, leak, or padded-leak.

DESIGN

A 4096-byte heap stores eight-byte headers and aligned payloads. Allocation uses
first fit and splits blocks; freeing merges adjacent free blocks. Initialization
is automatic. Failed allocations return NULL with a diagnostic; invalid frees
exit with status 2. An exit handler reports leaked payload bytes and objects.
malloc(0) fails, and free(NULL) is treated as invalid.

STRESS WORKLOAD

memgrind times 50 repetitions of the three required tasks plus alternating-hole
reuse and random mixed-size replacement. Every task frees its objects.

TEST PLAN

Check alignment and distinct byte patterns for overlap or corruption, reallocate
freed space to check reuse and coalescing, and verify full-heap recovery. Test
10,000 random operations, failed allocations, invalid frees, and exact leak
counts. Compare error messages, source locations, and exit statuses.

VERIFICATION

All tests passed on iLab2 with GCC 13.3 and locally with address and undefined
behavior sanitizers. p1.tar contains the clean P1 submission directory.
